1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,748
Slow drift is the most expensive failure mode in manufacturing.

2
00:00:04,833 --> 00:00:09,173
A Shewhart chart, limits at plus and minus three sigma.

3
00:00:09,266 --> 00:00:11,734
The process starts on target.

4
00:00:11,833 --> 00:00:14,613
The first twenty subgroups are noise.

5
00:00:14,700 --> 00:00:19,232
Then a drift begins, fifteen hundredths of a sigma per step.

6
00:00:19,316 --> 00:00:23,176
Every point still sits comfortably inside the limits.

7
00:00:23,266 --> 00:00:29,046
Each point is judged alone, then forgotten. The chart has no memory.

8
00:00:30,333 --> 00:00:33,353
A chart with a memory behaves differently.

9
00:00:33,450 --> 00:00:36,424
Its limits are calibrated first, so both

10
00:00:36,524 --> 00:00:39,422
charts share the same false alarm rate.

11
00:00:39,516 --> 00:00:42,248
Same centre line, same data.

12
00:00:42,333 --> 00:00:45,905
The faint points are the raw measurements from before.

13
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:49,047
This statistic weights the newest subgroup and

14
00:00:49,147 --> 00:00:51,852
carries forward what it has already seen.

15
00:00:51,950 --> 00:00:55,122
So evidence accumulates, and every subgroup

16
00:00:55,222 --> 00:00:57,178
after the drift adds to it.

17
00:00:57,266 --> 00:01:01,606
It crosses the limit while every raw point still looks innocent.

18
00:01:01,700 --> 00:01:06,795
At a one sigma drift: about ten subgroups instead of forty

19
00:01:06,895 --> 00:01:11,632
four. Four times sooner, at the same false alarm cost.

